"""Book 4 pass 2 (11 Oct 2026): full worked solutions, Chapters 1-10. S[chapter][problem] = text.
Format: Idea, the numbers, Check, The usual mistake."""
S = {}
S[1] = {
1: "Idea: the free-space wavelength is c/f, and in stripline the whole field sits in the dielectric, so the guided wavelength is shorter by √εr. λ0 = 2.998×10⁸/5.8×10⁹ = 5.17 cm. √3.55 = 1.884, so λg = 5.17/1.884 = 2.74 cm. Check: at 2.45 GHz Worked Example 1.1's numbers scale by 2.45/5.8, and 5.8 GHz is about 2.4 times higher, so a wavelength near 2.7 cm is about right. The usual mistake is to divide by εr instead of its square root.",
2: "Idea: a trace starts to behave as a transmission line when it reaches about a tenth of a guided wavelength, so set λg = 10 × 2 cm = 20 cm and solve for f. f = c/(√εr λg) = 2.998×10⁸/(1.884 × 0.20) = 0.80 GHz. Check: at 0.80 GHz the free-space wavelength is 37.5 cm and the stripline wavelength 37.5/1.884 = 19.9 cm, a tenth of which is 2 cm. The usual mistake is to use the free-space wavelength, which would put the limit at 1.5 GHz, nearly twice too high.",
3: "Idea: dBm is 10 log of power in milliwatts, and power in a resistor is V²/R. 27 dBm = 10^2.7 mW = 501 mW. −90 dBm = 10^(−9) mW = 10⁻¹² W, so V = √(PR) = √(10⁻¹² × 50) = 7.07 µV rms. Check: 0 dBm in 50 Ω is 224 mV rms, and −90 dB in power is −90 dB in voltage terms too, a factor of 31,600 down: 224 mV/31,600 = 7.08 µV. The usual mistake is to use 20 log for a power ratio.",
4: "Idea: a real capacitor is a series RLC; it is capacitive below self-resonance and inductive above. f = 1/(2π√(LC)) = 1/(2π√(0.6×10⁻⁹ × 100×10⁻¹²)) = 650 MHz. Check: at 650 MHz the reactance of 100 pF is 2.45 Ω and that of 0.6 nH is 2.45 Ω, equal and opposite. The usual mistake is to keep treating the part as a capacitor above 650 MHz, where it is an inductor.",
5: "Idea: in decibels gains add and losses subtract. −30 + 20 − 2 − 6 = −18 dBm. Check: in linear terms 1 µW × 100 × 0.631 × 0.251 = 15.8 µW, which is −18.0 dBm. The usual mistake is to add a loss as a positive gain because it is written as a positive number.",
6: "Idea: at series resonance the capacitive and inductive reactances cancel, leaving only the small ESR. A coupling or bypass capacitor chosen to resonate at the operating frequency is the lowest impedance any single part can offer there, a fraction of an ohm. Check: Problem 4's part has 2.45 Ω of reactance from each element at 650 MHz but only its ESR in total. The usual mistake is to choose the largest capacitance available, whose lower self-resonance makes it an inductor at the operating frequency.",
}
S[2] = {
1: "Idea: Z0 = √(L/C) and vp = 1/√(LC) give C = 1/(Z0 vp) and L = Z0/vp. vp = 0.85 × 2.998×10⁸ = 2.55×10⁸ m/s. C = 1/(75 × 2.55×10⁸) = 52.3 pF/m, L = 75/2.55×10⁸ = 294 nH/m. Check: √(L/C) = √(294×10⁻⁹/52.3×10⁻¹²) = 75.0 Ω and 1/√(LC) = 2.55×10⁸ m/s. The usual mistake is to use the free-space velocity, which gives values 15 % too small.",
2: "Idea: Γ = (ZL − Z0)/(ZL + Z0); VSWR, return loss and mismatch loss all follow from |Γ|. Γ = 25/125 = 0.20. VSWR = 1.2/0.8 = 1.5. RL = −20 log 0.20 = 14.0 dB. ML = −10 log(1 − 0.04) = 0.18 dB. Check: 4 % of the power is reflected and 96 % delivered, and −10 log 0.96 = 0.18 dB. The usual mistake is to confuse return loss, a large number for a good match, with mismatch loss, a small one.",
3: "Idea: the same formula, with a load below Z0. Γ = (25 − 50)/(25 + 50) = −0.333, so |Γ| = 0.333 and VSWR = 1.333/0.667 = 2.0. The negative sign means the load is below Z0, a voltage minimum at the load. Check: VSWR for a resistive load is the ratio of the larger resistance to the smaller, 50/25 = 2. The usual mistake is to report a negative VSWR or drop the sign of Γ, which carries the information about where the voltage minimum lies.",
4: "Idea: a quarter-wave line inverts the load about Z0: Zin = Z0²/ZL. Zin = 50²/200 = 12.5 Ω. Check: the normalized load 4 becomes 1/4, and 0.25 × 50 = 12.5 Ω. The usual mistake is to forget that the inversion holds only at the frequency where the line is a quarter wavelength.",
5: "Idea: an open stub shorter than a quarter wave is capacitive, Zin = −jZ0 cot βl. At one eighth of a wavelength βl = 45° and cot 45° = 1, so Zin = −j50 Ω. C = 1/(ωX) = 1/(2π × 2.45×10⁹ × 50) = 1.30 pF. Check: a 1.30 pF capacitor has 50 Ω of reactance at 2.45 GHz. The usual mistake is to use tan instead of cot, which describes a shorted stub and would give an inductor.",
6: "Idea: a line of length l rotates the reflection coefficient by 2βl, so the impedance seen depends on where it is measured. The same load reads differently at every plane along the line, and a number has meaning only at a stated plane, usually the package lead or the device pad. Check: 0.1λ of line rotates Γ by 72°, which can turn a resistive load into a strongly reactive one. The usual mistake is to copy a load-pull impedance from a data sheet without its reference plane and then add the board's line length twice.",
}
S[3] = {
1: "Idea: an L-network's Q is set by the impedance ratio, Q = √(Rhigh/Rlow − 1); the series element goes on the low side and the shunt element on the high side. Q = √(50/5 − 1) = 3.0. Series reactance Q × 5 = 15 Ω, L = 15/(2π × 900×10⁶) = 2.65 nH. Shunt reactance 50/3 = 16.7 Ω, C = 1/(2π × 900×10⁶ × 16.7) = 10.6 pF. Check: 5 + j15 Ω in parallel with −j16.7 Ω gives 50 Ω real. The usual mistake is to put the shunt element on the 5 Ω side, where it would have to be smaller than 5 Ω and the network could not reach 50 Ω.",
2: "Idea: the high-pass L swaps the element types: a shunt inductor and a series capacitor. Q = √(200/50 − 1) = √3 = 1.73. The shunt element goes across the higher resistance, the 200 Ω side, and the series capacitor faces the 50 Ω side. Check: a shunt reactance of 200/1.73 = 115 Ω reduces 200 Ω to a series equivalent of 50 Ω with 86.6 Ω of inductive reactance, which the series capacitor cancels. The usual mistake is to choose the side by element type rather than by resistance.",
3: "Idea: a pi network is two back-to-back L-networks meeting at a virtual resistance Rv = Rhigh/(Q² + 1), which must be below both terminations. Rv = 50/(8² + 1) = 50/65 = 0.77 Ω. Check: Rv is still below 10 Ω, so the 10 Ω side can be matched to it; raising Q from 5 to 8 lowered Rv from 1.92 Ω and narrowed the bandwidth. The usual mistake is to use Q² instead of Q² + 1.",
4: "Idea: a finite-Q inductor adds a series resistance X/QL, which appears in series with the low-side load and takes its share of the power. R = 15/30 = 0.5 Ω in series with 5 Ω, so the load receives 5/5.5 = 0.909 of the power, a loss of −10 log 0.909 = 0.41 dB. Check: the network Q is 3, and the loss estimate Q/QL = 3/30 = 10 % agrees. The usual mistake is to compare 0.5 Ω with 50 Ω; on the low side it is compared with 5 Ω, which is why high-ratio matches are lossy.",
5: "Idea: the network must present the series reactance that Worked Example 3.1 placed next to the load, 20 Ω, and the load already supplies part of it. The network supplies 20 − 8 = 12 Ω of inductive reactance, 0.78 nH at 2.45 GHz. Check: 10 + j8 + j12 = 10 + j20 Ω, the impedance Worked Example 3.1 matched. The usual mistake is to design for the resistive part alone and leave the load reactance to detune the match.",
6: "Idea: an L-network's Q, and with it the bandwidth and the loss in its parts, is fixed by the impedance ratio. Two sections, each transforming by the square root of the total ratio, each have a lower Q, so the cascade is wider and loses less. Check: for 50 to 5 Ω a single section has Q = 3.0; two sections through 15.8 Ω each have Q = √(3.16 − 1) = 1.47. The usual mistake is to think the second section doubles the loss; at half the Q each loses about half as much.",
}
S[4] = {
1: "Idea: a quarter-wave transformer's impedance is the geometric mean of the two it joins. Zq = √(100 × 50) = 70.7 Ω. Check: 70.7²/100 = 50 Ω. The usual mistake is to use the arithmetic mean, 75 Ω, which leaves Γ = 0.03 at the center frequency.",
2: "Idea: the single-section bandwidth formula depends on the ratio of the two impedances, so 100 to 50 Ω behaves as 25 to 50 Ω. Δf/f0 = 2 − (4/π) arccos[(Γm/√(1 − Γm²)) × 2√(Z0RL)/|RL − Z0|] = 2 − (4/π) arccos(0.1005 × 2.828) = 2 − (4/π)(1.282) = 0.367, or 36.7 %. Check: a ratio of 2 is mild, and a third of an octave of bandwidth at |Γ| < 0.1 is about what a single section gives. The usual mistake is to treat 100 to 50 Ω as a different problem from 25 to 50 Ω.",
3: "Idea: a shunt stub at the load works only if the load conductance already equals 1/Z0. YL = 1/(25 − j25) = (25 + j25)/1250 = 0.02 + j0.02 S. The conductance is 0.02 S = 1/50 Ω, so only the susceptance +j0.02 S must be cancelled. A shorted stub has Y = −j(1/Z0) cot βl, so cot βl = 1, βl = 45° and l = λ/8. Check: a λ/8 shorted stub has Zin = jZ0 tan 45° = j50 Ω, admittance −j0.02 S. The usual mistake is to work in impedances, where the stub's parallel connection does not add.",
4: "Idea: moving toward the generator by d rotates Γ by 2βd, clockwise on the chart. 2βd = 2 × 360° × 0.1 = 72°. Check: a full circle is half a wavelength, 0.5λ, so 0.1λ is a fifth of a circle, 72°. The usual mistake is to rotate by βd, 36°, forgetting the round trip.",
5: "Idea: a binomial transformer steps the logarithm of impedance in binomial proportions, so the section next to each end is closest to that end's impedance. For two sections from 25 to 50 Ω, ln Z1 = ln 25 + ln 2/4 and ln Z2 = ln 25 + 3 ln 2/4, giving Z1 = 29.7 Ω next to the 25 Ω load and Z2 = 42.0 Ω next to the 50 Ω line. Check: Z1 × Z2 = 1250 = 25 × 50, the symmetry every binomial design has. The usual mistake is to reverse the order, which puts the largest step at each end.",
6: "Idea: a line's reactance changes with frequency in proportion to its electrical length, so a short stub and a short line section drift less from their design values as frequency moves. Check: a 45° stub moves to 49.5° at +10 %, a 135° stub to 148.5°; the second's cot changes several times as much. The usual mistake is to choose the stub solution that is easier to lay out without looking at the bandwidth.",
}
S[5] = {
1: "Idea: return loss and insertion loss are −20 log of |S11| and |S21|, and a lossless two-port has |S11|² + |S21|² = 1. RL = −20 log 0.1 = 20 dB. IL = −20 log 0.995 = 0.04 dB. |S11|² + |S21|² = 0.010 + 0.990 = 1.000, so it is lossless within the precision given. Check: the 0.04 dB is the 1 % of power that is reflected, not absorbed. The usual mistake is to call 0.04 dB of insertion loss a dissipation; here it is all mismatch.",
2: "Idea: Z = Z0(1 + Γ)/(1 − Γ). Γ = 0.3∠−45° = 0.212 − j0.212. Zin = 50 (1.212 − j0.212)/(0.788 + j0.212) = 68.3 − j31.9 Ω. Check: the angle −45° puts the point in the lower half of the chart, capacitive, and |Γ| = 0.3 keeps it near the center, so a resistance above 50 Ω with modest negative reactance is right. The usual mistake is to drop the complex arithmetic and use |Γ| alone.",
3: "Idea: the error bounds of a mismatched source and load are 20 log(1 ± |ΓS||ΓL|). |ΓS||ΓL| = 0.3 × 0.2 = 0.06. 20 log 1.06 = +0.51 dB and 20 log 0.94 = −0.54 dB. Check: the bounds are not symmetric because the log is not; for small products both approach ±8.7 × 0.06 = ±0.52 dB. The usual mistake is to add the two return losses instead of multiplying the reflection coefficients.",
4: "Idea: Rollett's K = (1 − |S11|² − |S22|² + |Δ|²)/(2|S12S21|), and K > 1 with |Δ| < 1 means unconditional stability. K = (1 − 0.25 − 0.25 + 0.41²)/(2 × 0.1 × 2.0) = 0.668/0.4 = 1.67. With |Δ| = 0.41 < 1, the device is unconditionally stable. Check: Δ = S11S22 − S12S21 = 0.25∠−100° − 0.2∠130°, whose magnitude computes to 0.408. The usual mistake is to test K alone; K > 1 with |Δ| > 1 is not stable.",
5: "Idea: a reciprocal network has S12 = S21. S12 = 0.7∠−30°. Check: passive networks built of resistors, capacitors, inductors and lines are reciprocal; ferrites and transistors are not. The usual mistake is to conjugate the phase, which is the lossless condition on a different matrix product.",
6: "Idea: open and short circuits are hard to make at microwave frequencies, because an open radiates and has fringing capacitance and a short has inductance, and an active device terminated in them may oscillate. Matched loads are realizable and keep the device stable while it is measured, and waves are what the instruments measure. Check: every VNA calibration standard set includes a load. The usual mistake is to think S-parameters are only a convenience; for a transistor at 10 GHz the open-circuit measurement may not exist.",
}
S[6] = {
1: "Idea: scale the normalized prototype by impedance and frequency: C = g/(R0 ωc), L = g R0/ωc. For the third-order Butterworth, g1 = g3 = 1 and g2 = 2. ωc = 2π × 500×10⁶ = 3.14×10⁹ rad/s. C1 = C3 = 1/(50 × 3.14×10⁹) = 6.37 pF; L2 = 2 × 50/3.14×10⁹ = 31.8 nH. Check: at 500 MHz the 6.37 pF capacitors have 50 Ω of reactance and the inductor 100 Ω. The usual mistake is to forget the 2π and scale by f.",
2: "Idea: a Butterworth filter of order n has attenuation 10 log[1 + (f/fc)^(2n)]. At three times the cutoff, 10 log(1 + 3⁶) = 10 log 730 = 28.6 dB. Check: far beyond cutoff the slope is 6n = 18 dB per octave, and 3 is 1.58 octaves, giving 28.5 dB. The usual mistake is to use (f/fc)^n, which halves the decibels.",
3: "Idea: the midband loss of a coupled-resonator filter is about 4.343 Σg/(FBW Qu). For the 0.1 dB third-order prototype Σg = 1.0316 + 1.1474 + 1.0316 = 3.21. Loss = 4.343 × 3.21/(0.02 × 500) = 1.39 dB. Check: halving the bandwidth from Chapter 18's 4 % doubles the loss for the same Qu. The usual mistake is to use the unloaded Q of one resonator as if it set the loss alone; the narrower the filter, the more each resonator's loss counts.",
4: "Idea: λg = λ0/√(1 − (fc/f)²), with fc = 6.56 GHz for WR-90's 22.86 mm broad wall. λ0 = 2.998×10⁸/12×10⁹ = 24.98 mm. λg = 24.98/√(1 − (6.56/12)²) = 24.98/0.838 = 29.8 mm. Check: λg is always longer than λ0 in a hollow guide and approaches it far above cutoff. The usual mistake is to forget the square inside the root.",
5: "Idea: the TE10 cutoff is where the broad wall is half a wavelength, fc = c/(2a). fc = 2.998×10⁸/(2 × 0.0158) = 9.49 GHz. Check: 15.8 mm is the broad wall of WR-62, used from about 12.4 to 18 GHz, well above its 9.49 GHz cutoff. The usual mistake is to use the narrow wall.",
6: "Idea: the midband loss of a filter is inversely proportional to its resonators' Qu, and after a power amplifier that loss is heat. A cavity's Q is roughly ten times that of microstrip, so it dissipates about a tenth of the power, and its size handles the voltage and the heat. Check: Worked Example 18.2 gave 1.74 dB at Qu = 200 and 0.17 dB at Qu = 2000; at 100 W, 1.74 dB is 33 W of heat. The usual mistake is to size the filter for rejection alone.",
}
S[7] = {
1: "Idea: a practical half-wave dipole is cut about 4 % short of λ/2 to cancel its reactance; the problem gives 0.48λ. λ = 2.998×10⁸/5.8×10⁹ = 5.17 cm, so 0.48λ = 2.48 cm tip to tip. Check: half of 5.17 cm is 2.58 cm, and the shortened length sits a little below it. The usual mistake is to cut it to the full λ/2 and find the resonance a few percent low.",
2: "Idea: gain is efficiency times directivity, and efficiency is the radiation resistance's share of the total. η = 5/(5 + 3) = 0.625. G = 0.625 × 1.5 = 0.94, or 10 log 0.94 = −0.28 dBi. Check: 1.76 dBi of directivity minus 10 log(1/0.625) = 2.04 dB of loss gives −0.28 dBi. The usual mistake is to quote directivity as gain for a small antenna, where the loss resistance is comparable to the radiation resistance.",
3: "Idea: the path factor between isotropic antennas is (λ/4πR)². λ = 0.1224 m. (0.1224/(4π × 1000))² = (9.74×10⁻⁶)² = 9.48×10⁻¹¹, or −100.2 dB. Check: Worked Example 8.4 gave −80.2 dB at 100 m; ten times the distance adds 20 dB. The usual mistake is to drop the square and get half the decibels.",
4: "Idea: a dish's gain is ηap(πD/λ)² and its beamwidth about 70λ/D degrees. λ = 3.0 cm. G = 0.6 × (π × 0.3/0.03)² = 0.6 × 987 = 592, or 27.7 dBi. θ3dB ≈ 70 × 0.03/0.3 = 7.0°. Check: a common rule gives G ≈ 27,000/θ² for a pencil beam, 27,000/49 = 551, close to 592. The usual mistake is to use the radius in place of the diameter, which costs 6 dB.",
5: "Idea: effective aperture and gain are tied by Ae = Gλ²/(4π). G = 10^0.6 = 3.98. Ae = 3.98 × 0.1224²/(4π) = 4.74×10⁻³ m², about 47 cm². Check: an isotropic antenna at 2.45 GHz has λ²/4π = 11.9 cm², and 4 times that is 48 cm². The usual mistake is to use the gain in dBi directly in the formula.",
6: "Idea: a circular wave is the sum of two equal linear components at right angles, 90° apart in time. A linear antenna at any angle responds to one combination of them that carries exactly half the power, so the loss is 3 dB whatever its orientation. Check: if the two components have amplitude 1, a linear antenna at angle φ sees cos φ and sin φ in quadrature, whose powers sum to 1 of the total 2. The usual mistake is to try to recover the 3 dB by rotating the antenna.",
}
S[8] = {
1: "Idea: to steer to θ0 the progressive phase must be β = −kd sin θ0. With d = 0.5λ, kd = 180°, so β = −180° × sin 45° = −127.3°. Check: at θ0 = 90° the step would be −180°, endfire, and at 0° it is zero, broadside; 45° lies between. The usual mistake is to use the element spacing in meters without converting kd to degrees.",
2: "Idea: an array's gain is about the element gain plus 10 log N, because the power of N elements adds coherently in the beam direction. 6 + 10 log 64 = 6 + 18.1 = 24.1 dBi. Check: 64 elements is six doublings, 6 × 3 dB = 18 dB. The usual mistake is to use 20 log N, which describes the coherent voltage sum, not the gain: the power in the beam rises as N², but the power fed in rises as N too.",
3: "Idea: a uniform linear array's broadside beamwidth is about 0.886λ/(Nd) radians. 0.886/(32 × 0.5) = 0.0554 rad = 3.2°. Check: the aperture is 16λ long, and 51°/16 = 3.2°, the same rule written in degrees. The usual mistake is to forget that the beam broadens as 1/cos θ0 when it is steered away from broadside.",
4: "Idea: no grating lobe appears while d/λ < 1/(1 + |sin θmax|). For ±30°, d/λ < 1/(1 + 0.5) = 0.667. Check: at broadside only the limit is 1λ, and at ±90° it falls to 0.5λ. The usual mistake is to design for broadside and discover a grating lobe when the beam is steered.",
5: "Idea: a b-bit phase shifter has a step of 360°/2^b, and a uniformly distributed error of that width has σ = step/√12; the gain loss is about σ² in nepers, 4.343σ² dB with σ in radians. Step = 360°/32 = 11.25°. σ = 11.25°/√12 = 3.25° = 0.0567 rad. Loss = 4.343 × 0.0567² = 0.014 dB. Check: each extra bit cuts the loss by four, so 4 bits would give 0.056 dB. The usual mistake is to treat the quantization error as a beam-pointing error; with random rounding it is a small loss and a raised sidelobe floor.",
6: "Idea: with fixed apertures each antenna's gain rises as f² and the path factor falls as 1/f², so the link factor rises as f². Change = 20 log(60/2.45) = 27.8 dB in favor of 60 GHz. At 100 m, oxygen absorption of about 15 dB/km at 60 GHz takes back 1.5 dB, leaving about 26 dB. Check: Worked Example 8.4 found 21 dB for 28/2.45, and 20 log(60/28) = 6.6 dB more gives 27.8 dB. The usual mistake is to compare fixed-gain antennas, for which the higher frequency loses 20 log of the ratio instead.",
}
S[9] = {
1: "Idea: a Wilkinson divider uses quarter-wave lines of √2 Z0 and a resistor of 2Z0. √2 × 75 = 106.1 Ω lines and a 150 Ω resistor. Check: the two 106.1 Ω arms, each transforming the 75 Ω output to 106.1²/75 = 150 Ω, appear in parallel as 75 Ω at the input. The usual mistake is to keep the 50 Ω values of 70.7 Ω and 100 Ω.",
2: "Idea: each two-way stage halves the power, so N equal stages give 2^N outputs at 3 dB per stage. An eight-way divider is three stages: 3 × 3.01 = 9.03 dB, or 10 log 8. Check: each output carries 1/8 of the input power, 12.5 %. The usual mistake is to call this a loss; it is the division the network is built for, and dissipation comes on top.",
3: "Idea: isolation is coupling plus directivity in decibels. 20 + 25 = 45 dB. Check: the isolated port sees the input signal 45 dB down, while the coupled port sees it 20 dB down, and the 25 dB difference is the directivity. The usual mistake is to think directivity and isolation are the same number.",
4: "Idea: finite directivity adds a leakage term of 10^(−D/20) to the true reflection coefficient with unknown phase. True Γ = 10^(−20/20) = 0.1; leakage 10^(−25/20) = 0.0562. The reading lies between 0.1 + 0.0562 = 0.156, a return loss of 16.1 dB, and 0.1 − 0.0562 = 0.044, a return loss of 27.2 dB. Check: the error is asymmetric, larger toward a falsely good reading. The usual mistake is to trust a return loss measured within 10 dB of the coupler's directivity.",
5: "Idea: in a balanced amplifier the input hybrid splits the signal with a 90° difference. Each amplifier's reflection makes a second pass through the hybrid, so the two reflections arrive at the input port 180° apart and cancel, and at the isolated port in phase, where its load absorbs them. Check: if each amplifier has Γ = 0.3, the input port sees zero reflection in the ideal case and the isolated load absorbs 9 % of the power. The usual mistake is to think the amplifiers must be matched individually; only their reflections must be equal.",
6: "Idea: the scattering matrix of a lossless network is unitary, and of a reciprocal one symmetric. For three ports with all diagonal terms zero, the unitarity conditions force at least two of the three transmission terms to be zero, which contradicts the remaining conditions, so no such network exists. A matched three-port must be lossy, like the Wilkinson with its resistor, or nonreciprocal, like the circulator. Check: the Wilkinson is matched at all three ports precisely because its resistor can absorb power. The usual mistake is to look for a cleverer lossless T-junction.",
}
S[10] = {
1: "Idea: Hammerstad's simple formula: εeff = (εr + 1)/2 + (εr − 1)/2 × (1 + 12/u)^(−1/2), with u = W/h. (3.55 + 1)/2 = 2.275 and (3.55 − 1)/2 = 1.275. (1 + 12/2.24)^(−1/2) = (6.357)^(−1/2) = 0.397. εeff = 2.275 + 1.275 × 0.397 = 2.78. Check: εeff must lie between (εr + 1)/2 = 2.28 and εr = 3.55, nearer the top for a wide line. The usual mistake is to use εr for microstrip, whose field is partly in air.",
2: "Idea: λg = λ0/√εeff. λ0 = 2.998×10⁸/28×10⁹ = 10.7 mm. λg = 10.7/√2.78 = 10.7/1.667 = 6.42 mm. Check: a quarter wave at 28 GHz is then 1.6 mm, which is why millimeter-wave matching elements are drawn in fractions of a millimeter. The usual mistake is to use εr = 3.55, giving 5.68 mm.",
3: "Idea: the wide-strip stripline formula W/b = 30π/(√εr Z0) − 0.441, valid for W/b above about 0.35. 30π/(√3 × 50) = 94.25/86.6 = 1.088, minus 0.441 gives 0.647, so W = 0.647 × 0.8 mm = 0.52 mm. Check: W/b = 0.647 is inside the formula's range. The usual mistake is to use a microstrip formula with b as the substrate height.",
4: "Idea: dielectric loss per unit length is proportional to frequency at a constant loss tangent, because αd ∝ f √εeff tan δ. 2 × 0.037 = 0.074 dB/cm at 20 GHz. Check: conductor loss, by contrast, rises only as √f from skin effect, so at high frequency the dielectric term takes the larger share. The usual mistake is to scale the dielectric loss by √f.",
5: "Idea: stripline's field lies wholly within the homogeneous dielectric between its two ground planes, so the wave sees only εr, and εeff = εr. Check: the stripline wavelength in Chapter 1 used √εr directly. The usual mistake is to apply microstrip's partial-filling formula to stripline.",
6: "Idea: on a thick substrate, surface waves and higher-order modes start at lower frequencies, and a 50 Ω line becomes wide compared with the structures it must connect. Thin substrates push those modes above the operating frequency and keep 50 Ω traces narrow enough for millimeter-wave pads and bumps. Check: a 50 Ω line is about twice as wide as the substrate is thick on common laminates, so a 0.1 mm core gives a trace of about 0.2 mm. The usual mistake is to keep a 0.8 mm substrate and find the traces wider than the chip's pads.",
}
S[11] = {
1: "Idea: Q = √(Rhigh/Rlow − 1). Q = √(50/10 − 1) = 2. Check: this is the Q of Worked Example 3.1, whose 10 dB bandwidth was 38 %, about 1/Q in the loose sense this chapter uses. The usual mistake is to invert the ratio and take the square root of a negative number.",
2: "Idea: a two-section binomial transformer steps ln Z by a quarter, a half and a quarter of the total. Ratio 4, so the steps are 4^(1/4) = 1.414 each in the outer sections: Z1 = 50 × 1.414 = 70.7 Ω and Z2 = 50 × 4^(3/4) = 141.4 Ω. Check: Z1 × Z2 = 10,000 = 50 × 200. The usual mistake is to space the impedances linearly, 100 and 150 Ω.",
3: "Idea: Bode-Fano for a parallel RC load: the best flat in-band reflection over a band Δω is Γ = exp(−π/(Δω RC)). RC = 50 × 2×10⁻¹² = 100 ps. Δω RC = 2π × 2×10⁹ × 10⁻¹⁰ = 1.257, so the exponent is π/1.257 = 2.5 and Γ = e^(−2.5) = 0.082, a return loss of 21.7 dB. Check: the chapter's 50 Ω ∥ 5 pF load reached 17 dB across 1 GHz with exponent 2; this load stores less energy and does better over twice the band. The usual mistake is to think a better network beats the limit; no lossless network can.",
4: "Idea: rearrange Bode-Fano for bandwidth: Δf = 1/(2RC ln(1/Γ)). For 10 dB, Γ = 0.316 and ln(1/Γ) = 1.151. Δf = 1/(2 × 10⁻¹⁰ × 1.151) = 4.34 GHz. Check: put back into Problem 3's form, Δω RC = 2π × 4.34×10⁹ × 10⁻¹⁰ = 2.73 and exp(−π/2.73) = 0.316. The usual mistake is to forget the 2π between ω and f.",
5: "Idea: a binomial transformer is maximally flat, with all its reflection zeros at the center frequency. A Chebyshev transformer accepts a controlled ripple and spreads those zeros across the band, so the same number of sections holds |Γ| below the ripple level over a wider band. Check: the same trade appears in filters, where Chebyshev beats Butterworth in selectivity for a stated ripple. The usual mistake is to think the Chebyshev design is worse because it is not perfect at the center.",
6: "Idea: Bode-Fano limits a lumped reactance matched by a network. A distributed amplifier absorbs each transistor's capacitance into the shunt capacitance of an artificial transmission line, whose characteristic impedance √(L/C) is resistive up to the line's cutoff, so no single capacitance has to be matched. Its bandwidth is then set by the cutoff of the line sections. Check: the gains of the devices add rather than multiply, which is the price of the bandwidth. The usual mistake is to think the distributed amplifier breaks the limit; it changes the network the limit applies to.",
}
S[12] = {
1: "Idea: f101 = (c/2)√(1/a² + 1/d²) with a = 22.86 mm for WR-90. 1/a² = 1914 m⁻², 1/d² = 1600 m⁻². f = 1.499×10⁸ × √3514 = 1.499×10⁸ × 59.3 = 8.89 GHz. Check: the guide's cutoff is 6.56 GHz, and closing it at 25 mm raises the resonance above that, as it must. The usual mistake is to use the narrow wall b in place of a.",
2: "Idea: QL = Qu/(1 + β), and critical coupling means β = 1. QL = 5000/2 = 2500. Check: at critical coupling the external circuit dissipates as much as the resonator, so the loaded bandwidth doubles. The usual mistake is to assume the loaded Q equals the unloaded Q at a match.",
3: "Idea: the same loss formula as Chapter 6: 4.343 Σg/(FBW Qu). 4.343 × 5/(0.005 × 10,000) = 21.7/50 = 0.43 dB. Check: a 0.5 % filter with microstrip resonators at Qu = 200 would lose 21.7 dB, which is why narrow filters need cavities. The usual mistake is to enter the bandwidth as 0.5 instead of 0.005.",
4: "Idea: wall loss is set by the surface resistance Rs = √(ωμ0/2σ), so Q ∝ 1/Rs ∝ √σ. A quarter of copper's conductivity halves Q: 6000 × √0.25 = 3000. Check: silver plating gains only √(6.3/5.8) = 1.04 over copper, about 4 %. The usual mistake is to scale Q linearly with conductivity.",
5: "Idea: a closed metal cavity encloses its fields completely, so energy can leave only through the walls, the dielectric, or the coupling ports. There is no radiation term in its Q. Check: an open microstrip resonator does radiate, and that loss is part of why its Q is near 200. The usual mistake is to add a radiation term for a closed cavity with small coupling holes; at the holes it is part of the external Q.",
6: "Idea: a resonator's size scales with the wavelength of the wave it stores. Electromagnetic waves at 2 GHz have wavelengths of centimeters; acoustic waves at sound speeds of a few thousand to about 11,000 m/s have wavelengths of a few micrometers, tens of thousands of times smaller. Handsets therefore use the acoustic filters of Chapter 47. Check: c/v = 3×10⁸/4000 = 75,000 for a surface wave on lithium tantalate. The usual mistake is to think the obstacle is Q; cavities have plenty of it but are far too large.",
}
S[13] = {
1: "Idea: a quarter-wave monopole over a ground plane is λ/4 long. λ = 2.998×10⁸/868×10⁶ = 0.3454 m, so λ/4 = 86.3 mm. Check: at 915 MHz the same antenna is 81.9 mm, the familiar 8 cm whip of a LoRa module. The usual mistake is to cut it to λ/2, which is a dipole's total length.",
2: "Idea: Chu's limit, Qmin = 1/(ka)³ + 1/(ka), with a the sphere's radius. k = 2π/0.3454 = 18.2 rad/m, so ka = 18.2 × 0.030 = 0.546. Qmin = 1/0.163 + 1/0.546 = 6.14 + 1.83 = 8.0. Check: Worked Example 13.1's 20 mm radius at 915 MHz gave ka = 0.384 and Q = 20.3; a larger sphere at a slightly lower frequency gives the lower Q expected. The usual mistake is to use the diameter as a, which here would give ka = 0.273 and Q = 53.",
3: "Idea: an aperture's gain is ηap 4πA/λ². λ = 1.50 cm at 20 GHz. G = 0.51 × 4π × 0.050 × 0.040/0.0150² = 57.0, or 17.6 dBi. Check: this horn is half the size in each dimension of a horn at 10 GHz with the same efficiency, so its area in square wavelengths, and its gain, are unchanged. The usual mistake is to think a smaller horn must have less gain; gain depends on size in wavelengths.",
4: "Idea: a small loop's radiation resistance is proportional to the square of its area in square wavelengths, Rrad ∝ (A/λ²)². Area goes as diameter squared, so Rrad goes as the fourth power of the diameter. Check: halving the diameter of a small loop divides its radiation resistance by 16, which is why tiny loops in key fobs have efficiencies of a few percent. The usual mistake is to scale it with the area, a square law.",
5: "Idea: the ground plane acts as a mirror, so a monopole and its image form a dipole that radiates only into the upper half-space. The same power fills half the solid angle, so the directivity doubles: 2 × 1.64 = 3.28, or 5.15 dBi, against 2.15 dBi for the dipole. Check: the monopole's radiation resistance is half the dipole's, 36.5 Ω against 73 Ω, for the same reason. The usual mistake is to expect the gain on a small ground plane, which tilts the beam upward and loses most of the advantage.",
6: "Idea: a small antenna's Q, and so its bandwidth, is limited by Chu's bound for a lossless antenna. Adding loss lowers the total Q, Q = Qrad η, and widens the bandwidth beyond the lossless limit, at the cost of radiating less of the power. Check: an antenna at twice Chu's minimum Q with 50 % efficiency has a total Q equal to the minimum and the bandwidth of an ideal one. The usual mistake is to read a small product antenna's wide bandwidth as good design rather than as loss.",
}
S[14] = {
1: "Idea: the first Fresnel zone radius is r1 = √(λ d1 d2/(d1 + d2)). λ = 1.25 cm at 24 GHz, and at the midpoint d1 = d2 = 1000 m. r1 = √(0.0125 × 1000 × 1000/2000) = √6.25 = 2.50 m. Check: the radius grows as √d and falls as √f; at 6 GHz the same path would need 5.0 m of clearance. The usual mistake is to use the full path length in place of d1d2/(d1 + d2).",
2: "Idea: beyond the breakpoint dbp = 4hthr/λ, the two-ray loss steepens to 40 dB per decade. λ = 8.57 cm at 3.5 GHz. dbp = 4 × 30 × 2/0.0857 = 2.80 km. Check: raising the base-station antenna to 60 m would double the breakpoint to 5.6 km. The usual mistake is to omit the factor 4.",
3: "Idea: free-space loss is 20 log(4πd/λ), and gaseous absorption adds a loss per kilometer. λ = 5.00 mm at 60 GHz. 20 log(4π × 500/0.005) = 20 log(1.257×10⁶) = 122.0 dB. Oxygen adds 15 × 0.5 = 7.5 dB, for 129.5 dB. Check: 500 m at 60 GHz has 60/2.45 = 24.5 times the frequency of a 2.45 GHz link, so it loses 27.8 dB more in free space, as Problem 6 of Chapter 8 found. The usual mistake is to apply the 15 dB/km to the whole path in decibels per 100 m.",
4: "Idea: at grazing incidence the Fresnel-Kirchhoff parameter ν = 0, and the knife-edge loss there is 6.0 dB because half of the first Fresnel zone is blocked. Check: J(0) = 6.9 + 20 log(√(0.01 + 1) − 0.1) = 6.9 + 20 log 0.905 = 6.9 − 0.87 = 6.0 dB from the chapter's approximation. The usual mistake is to think a path that barely clears the obstacle by line of sight is free of loss; it needs about 60 % of the first zone clear.",
5: "Idea: rain attenuation per kilometer is kR^α with the ITU-R coefficients for the frequency and polarization. At 30 GHz horizontal, k = 0.2403 and α = 0.9485. 0.2403 × 25^0.9485 = 0.2403 × 21.2 = 5.09 dB/km, so 10.2 dB over 2 km. Check: Worked Example 14.3 used the same coefficients. The usual mistake is to forget that real rain cells are smaller than long paths, which ITU-R handles with a path reduction factor; at 2 km it is close to 1.",
6: "Idea: beyond the breakpoint the direct and reflected waves nearly cancel, and the small residual depends on the path difference 2hthr/d, measured in wavelengths. The received power is Pt Gt Gr (hthr)²/d⁴: the λ² of the free-space factor cancels against the 1/λ² from the phase difference squared. Check: in the formula, doubling either antenna height adds 6 dB, while frequency does not appear. The usual mistake is to apply the frequency-independent result inside the breakpoint, where free-space behavior still holds.",
}
S[15] = {
1: "Idea: the gyromagnetic resonance of a ferrite is f = γB, with γ = 28 GHz/T for the electron. f = 28 × 0.05 = 1.4 GHz. Check: a circulator biased below resonance at 0.05 T works above about 2 GHz, and one biased above resonance works below 1.4 GHz. The usual mistake is to operate a device near the resonance itself, where the ferrite's loss peaks.",
2: "Idea: |Γ| = (VSWR − 1)/(VSWR + 1) and the reflected fraction is |Γ|². |Γ| = 1/3, so 1/9 = 11.1 % of the forward power returns. Check: the return loss is 9.5 dB, and 10^(−0.95) = 0.112. The usual mistake is to think 2:1 means half the power is reflected.",
3: "Idea: an isolator returns reflected power to the source attenuated by its isolation, so the source sees |Γ|eff = |ΓL| × 10^(−I/20). With |ΓL| = 1 and 25 dB isolation, |Γ|eff = 10^(−1.25) = 0.056. Check: a 0.056 reflection is a 25 dB return loss and a VSWR of 1.12, which the amplifier tolerates easily. The usual mistake is to subtract the isolation from the return loss in voltage terms twice.",
4: "Idea: an ideal circulator routes each port to the next in its sense of rotation. Power entering port 2 leaves at port 3, and none reaches port 1. Check: in a radar front end the transmitter enters port 1, the antenna is port 2, and the echo entering port 2 goes to the receiver at port 3. The usual mistake is to think port 3 is isolated from every input.",
5: "Idea: the reflected power is the forward power reduced by the return loss, and the circulator routes it from the antenna port to the receiver port. 33 − 12 = 21 dBm, 126 mW, arrives at the receiver, plus any direct leakage. Check: 12 dB return loss means 6.3 % reflected, 0.063 × 2 W = 126 mW. The usual mistake is to count only the circulator's isolation, which protects against the direct path, not against the antenna's own reflection.",
6: "Idea: the isolator's termination absorbs all reflected power. If the antenna is disconnected, shorted or iced, all the forward power is reflected and routed to the termination, which must survive it continuously. Check: a 100 W amplifier into an open circuit puts 100 W into the load. The usual mistake is to rate the load for the reflection at the normal VSWR, 11 W at 2:1, and lose it on the first fault.",
}
S[16] = {
1: "Idea: the far-field distance is 2D²/λ. λ = 6.0 cm at 5 GHz. 2 × 0.3²/0.06 = 3.0 m. Check: at this distance the phase error across the aperture is π/8, 22.5°. The usual mistake is to use D instead of D².",
2: "Idea: the same formula. λ = 5.0 mm at 60 GHz. 2 × 0.1²/0.005 = 4.0 m. Check: a third of the diameter of Problem 1 at twelve times the frequency gives a slightly larger distance, as 12/9 = 1.33 predicts. The usual mistake is to assume a small antenna always has a short far-field distance; at millimeter waves a 10 cm array needs meters.",
3: "Idea: the gain-substitution method: replace the reference antenna with the antenna under test and add the difference in received power to the reference gain. G = 20.0 + (−43.5 − (−40.0)) = 16.5 dBi. Check: nothing else in the link changed, so only the gain difference can explain the 3.5 dB. The usual mistake is to forget to correct for a different mismatch between the two antennas.",
4: "Idea: the path factor is (λ/4πR)². λ = 3.0 cm. 20 log(0.03/(4π × 10)) = 20 log(2.39×10⁻⁴) = −72.4 dB. Check: at 1 m it would be −52.4 dB, and ten times the range costs 20 dB. The usual mistake is to use 10 log on a voltage-like ratio.",
5: "Idea: the two-antenna method: with identical antennas, Pr/Pt = G² × path factor, so 2G(dBi) = −52.4 − (−72.4) = 20.0 dB and G = 10.0 dBi each. Check: 10 + 10 − 72.4 = −52.4 dB. The usual mistake is to assign the whole 20 dB to one antenna.",
6: "Idea: a planar near-field scanner samples the field on a grid and transforms it to the far field; the samples must be no more than half a wavelength apart to avoid aliasing in angle. Check: at 10 GHz that is 15 mm, and at 60 GHz 2.5 mm, which sets the scan time. The usual mistake is to sample coarser to save time and fold energy from wide angles into the main beam.",
}
S[17] = {
1: "Idea: Te = T0(F − 1). F = 10^0.2 = 1.585, so Te = 290 × 0.585 = 169.6 K. Check: Chapter 25's 3 dB gives 288.6 K, so 2 dB should be a little over half of that. The usual mistake is to use the noise figure in decibels in the formula.",
2: "Idea: invert the relation: F = 1 + Te/T0. F = 1 + 75/290 = 1.259, so NF = 10 log 1.259 = 1.00 dB. Check: Chapter 25 lists 1 dB as 75.1 K. The usual mistake is to forget the 1 and take 10 log(75/290).",
3: "Idea: a matched attenuator at physical temperature T has Te = (L − 1)T. L = 10^0.3 = 1.995. Te = 0.995 × 290 = 288.6 K. Check: at T0 the attenuator's noise figure equals its loss, 3 dB, and a 3 dB noise figure is 288.6 K. The usual mistake is to say a passive part adds no noise.",
4: "Idea: N = kTB, and kT0 is −174 dBm/Hz. −174 + 10 log 10⁶ = −174 + 60 = −114.0 dBm. Check: kTB = 1.381×10⁻²³ × 290 × 10⁶ = 4.00×10⁻¹⁵ W = −114.0 dBm. The usual mistake is to use 20 log for the bandwidth.",
5: "Idea: loss at 290 K ahead of an amplifier adds its decibels to the noise figure, since F = L × Famp. 1.5 + 0.8 = 2.3 dB. Check: in linear terms 1.413 × 1.202 = 1.698, which is 2.30 dB. The usual mistake is to add the cable's noise after dividing by a gain it does not have.",
6: "Idea: Tout = Tin/L + (1 − 1/L)T. L = 1.995. Tout = 10/1.995 + (1 − 0.501) × 290 = 5.0 + 144.7 = 149.7 K. Check: an attenuator moves its output halfway toward its own temperature for 3 dB, from 10 K toward 290 K, which lands near 150 K. The usual mistake is to subtract the loss in kelvin as if it were decibels.",
}
S[18] = {
1: "Idea: Qe = g0g1/FBW, with g0 = 1 and g1 = 1.0316 for the 0.1 dB third-order prototype. Qe = 1.0316/0.05 = 20.6. Check: Worked Example 18.1 gave 25.8 at 4 %, and a wider filter needs proportionally heavier loading. The usual mistake is to use the Butterworth g1 = 1.",
2: "Idea: M12 = FBW/√(g1g2). M12 = 0.05/√(1.0316 × 1.1474) = 0.05/1.088 = 0.0460. Check: 4 % gave 0.0368, and the coupling scales with bandwidth, 0.0368 × 1.25 = 0.0460. The usual mistake is to use the product g1g2 without the root.",
3: "Idea: a half-wave resonator is λg/2 = c/(2f√εeff). c/(2 × 5×10⁹ × √2.79) = 2.998×10⁸/(1.670×10¹⁰) = 1.80 cm, before end-effect corrections. Check: Section 18.3 found 3.66 cm at 2.45 GHz, and 5.0 GHz is a little over twice that frequency. The usual mistake is to forget that the open ends' fringing makes the line look longer, so the drawn length must be a little shorter.",
4: "Idea: loss ≈ 4.343 Σg/(FBW Qu), with Σg = 3.2106. 4.343 × 3.2106/(0.04 × 500) = 13.94/20 = 0.70 dB. Check: Worked Example 18.2 gave 1.74 dB at Qu = 200, and 2.5 times the Q divides it by 2.5. The usual mistake is to use a fractional bandwidth in percent.",
5: "Idea: the bandpass transformation maps f to Ω = (1/FBW)(f/f0 − f0/f). f/f0 = 2.30/2.45 = 0.9388 and f0/f = 1.0652, so the difference is −0.1264 and Ω = −0.1264/0.04 = −3.16, |Ω| = 3.16. Check: the lower stopband is a little more selective than the upper at the same offset, since 2.60 GHz, 150 MHz above, gave 2.97. The usual mistake is to use the arithmetic offset 150/98 = 1.53, which ignores the geometric symmetry of a bandpass filter.",
6: "Idea: A = 10 log[1 + ε² cosh²(n arccosh|Ω|)], with ε² = 0.0233 and n = 3. arccosh 3.16 = 1.818, so cosh(5.455) = 117.0. A = 10 log(1 + 0.0233 × 13,690) = 10 log 320 = 25.0 dB. Check: 2.60 GHz, with |Ω| = 2.97, gave 23.4 dB, and the lower side with the larger |Ω| gives a little more. The usual mistake is to use cos in place of cosh outside the passband.",
}
S[19] = {
1: "Idea: a series element Z between matched 50 Ω ports has IL = 20 log|1 + Z/2Z0|. IL = 20 log(1 + 2/100) = 0.17 dB. Check: Worked Example 19.1's 1 Ω gave 0.086 dB, and the loss is close to proportional to R while it is small. The usual mistake is to compare R with 50 Ω instead of 100 Ω, the source and load in series.",
2: "Idea: the off-state capacitance's reactance sets the isolation, 20 log|1 + Z/2Z0| with Z = 1/(jωC). X = 1/(2π × 5.8×10⁹ × 0.1×10⁻¹²) = 274 Ω, so |1 − j2.74| = 2.92 and the isolation is 9.3 dB. Check: at 2.45 GHz it was 16.4 dB, and isolation falls about 6 dB per octave as the reactance falls. The usual mistake is to quote a switch's isolation at a lower frequency than it is used at.",
3: "Idea: a shunt element Z gives S21 = 2Z/(2Z + Z0). |S21| = 4/(4 + 50) = 0.0741, an isolation of 22.6 dB. Check: Worked Example 19.2's 1 Ω gave 28.3 dB, and doubling R costs about 6 dB. The usual mistake is to think the shunt switch isolates by absorbing power; it mostly reflects it.",
}
